Subject: [PATCH] Input: nt36523 - preserve GPIO-to-IRQ lookup errors

A negative return from gpiod_to_irq() passes the existing nonzero test
and reaches request_threaded_irq() as an unsigned IRQ number. This can
replace -EPROBE_DEFER with a different error instead of deferring probe.

Reject negative mapping results before requesting an interrupt. Preserve
the original errno and use the existing input-device cleanup path, which
does not free an IRQ that was never acquired.

Apply after mainline-port/patches/0002-input-touchscreen-add-nt36523-spi-pen.patch.
The Linux v6.12.114 GPIO implementation returns positive IRQs or negative
errors; zero from a GPIO chip is converted to -ENXIO there. Valid IRQ
requests, GPIO polarity, reset sequencing and input geometry are unchanged.

diff --git a/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36523/nt36xxx.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36523/nt36xxx.c
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36523/nt36xxx.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36523/nt36xxx.c
@@ -1552,6 +1552,11 @@
 
 	//---set int-pin & request irq---
 	client->irq = gpiod_to_irq(ts->irq_gpio);
+	if (client->irq < 0) {
+		ret = dev_err_probe(&client->dev, client->irq,
+				    "failed to map interrupt GPIO\n");
+		goto err_int_request_failed;
+	}
 	if (client->irq) {
 		NVT_LOG("int_trigger_type=%d\n", ts->int_trigger_type);
 		ts->irq_enabled = true;
